  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 AM MDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nIt was a typical monsoon day yesterday, with some areas, mainly in \nsouthwest and in Johnson and Natrona Counties getting a LOT of rain, \ncausing some local flooding. Other areas saw nothing. And we \ncontinue to have some showers and embedded thunder around late \ntonight, although intensity is significantly less. And these will \ncontinue through the night, gradually becoming weaker and less \nnumerous.\n\nWe will have another round of showers and storms today. There are \nsome differences. For one, precipitable water values are less today \nby about 20 percent in most locations. This should bring less \ncoverage and somewhat lesser rainfall rates. The other difference is \nthat there will be more steering flow today,  generally about 10 to \n15 knots at 700 millibars and 25 to 35 knots at 500 millibars. So \nstorms should have more movement. This should decrease the chance of \nflooding somewhat. We still have an excessive rainfall outlook \nacross the entire area though, so the threat is still there. There \nis also the risk of strong to severe thunderstorms. Most of the area \nis under a marginal risk of severe weather today. There could be \nsome small hail, but this not the main risk. The main risk looks to \nbe strong wind gusts. Roughly north and west of a Buffalo to \nRiverton to Lyman line has a Conditional 1 outlook for wind, or \npossible convective wind gusts to 75 mph. Soundings do show some \ninverted V profiles, especially across northwestern Wyoming where \nthe surface will be drier, with low and mid level lapse rates close \nto 9 C/Km. This could lead to some strong downburst winds later \ntoday. Again, the best chance would be across the northwest but most \nareas will have a chance. And, like previous nights, most convection \nshould end after sunset but a few showers and storms will likely \nlinger through the night. As for temperatures, they should be a few \ndegrees warmer than yesterday with more sunshine expected. \n\nThe rest of the week looks like more of the same as the annoying \nmonsoonal moisture will hang around the area through at least \nThursday and possibly into Friday as well. The models continue to \nhave different ideas on which day will be more active with no real \nwell defined shortwaves with changes from day to day. Precipitable \nwater generally average about 125 to 150 percent of normal through \nthe period though. So, at this point we went with a generic solution \nof mainly diurnal showers and storms each afternoon. Temperatures \nshould remain fairly similar through the period, not very hot but \naveraging around 4 to 8 degrees above normal on average, with the \npossibility of a cooler day if there is more cloud cover than \nexpected. Drier air may then work in for next weekend and bring a \nreturn to drier weather but uncertainty remains high on the exact \ntiming. So, all in all, it continues to look like more of the same \nas the summer of the monsoon lives on in western and central \nWyoming.",
